@page "/docs"
@attribute [Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization.AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>User docs — CalCrony</PageTitle>

<p class="meta-mono text-uppercase mb-2">User documentation</p>
<h1>Using CalCrony</h1>
<p class="lead text-body-secondary" style="max-width:58ch">
    Everything works from Discord; this web app adds a friendlier view of the same data.
    Sign in with Discord and you'll see exactly the servers you share with the bot.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4 mb-2">First steps for server admins</h2>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    Right after inviting the bot, run these two commands once — they shape everything else:
</p>
<ol class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-2">
        <code>/settings server-timezone</code> — pick your zone from the command's suggestions
        (start typing a city, e.g. <code>America/Chicago</code>). Natural-language times
        ("tomorrow 6pm") are parsed in this zone; until it's set, the server runs on <b>UTC</b>,
        so local wall-clock times can land hours off or be rejected as "in the past". (Members
        can also set a personal <code>/settings timezone</code>, which wins over the server's
        for their own inputs.)
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <code>/settings default-channel</code> pointing at your events channel. Events, polls,
        and reminders created from this web app post their embeds there — <b>web creation is
        blocked until it's set</b>. Bot commands aren't affected (they post where they're run).
    </li>
</ol>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    Optional third step: <code>/settings native-events on</code> mirrors events into the server's
    built-in Events tab (the bot must hold <b>Manage Events</b>; existing events mirror when next
    edited). Check everything anytime with <code>/settings view</code> or from the server's
    settings page here.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4 mb-2">Set up your interest roles</h2>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    Several features point at Discord roles, so a few minutes in <b>Server Settings → Roles</b>
    pays off. Two patterns matter, and they behave very differently:
</p>
<ul class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Interest roles</b> — standing groups people join once and keep, e.g.
        <code>@@dnd</code>, <code>@@movie-night</code>, <code>@@raiders</code>. Let members
        self-assign them (Discord's onboarding prompts or a roles channel work great). These are
        what you point <i>planning</i> features at:
        <code>/availability role role:@@dnd</code> shows the whole group's free/busy grid before
        you pick a session time, and <code>/notify mention:@@dnd</code> pings the group before an
        event starts.
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Attendee roles</b> — temporary, per-event badges CalCrony manages for you, e.g.
        <code>@@session-players</code>. Create the role empty and pass it at
        <code>/create attendee-role:</code> — everyone who RSVPs <b>Going</b> gets it
        automatically, switching away or removing the RSVP removes it (unless, with multiple RSVPs
        on, another option they still hold grants the same role), and the whole role empties when
        the event ends. Perfect for pinging only confirmed attendees or temporarily unlocking a
        planning channel.
        <br />
        Each RSVP option can hand out a <i>different</i> role: mention one inside
        <code>rsvp-options</code> and whoever picks that option gets that role. So
        <code>rsvp-options:"🛡️ Tank x2 @@tank, 💚 Healer x2 @@healer, ⚔️ DPS x6 @@dps"</code>
        sorts a raid into three roles from one event, and switching from Tank to Healer swaps
        the roles over automatically (with multiple RSVPs on, a member holding both seats holds
        both roles).
    </li>
    <li class="mb-2">
        <b>Restricting signup by role</b> — the third use of interest roles: only their holders
        may RSVP. <code>/create restrict-to:@@raiders</code> limits every option to
        <code>@@raiders</code> (up to five roles, any one of them will do), and inside
        <code>rsvp-options</code> the word <code>only:</code> limits a single option —
        <code>rsvp-options:"🛡️ Tank x2 @@tank only: @@raiders, ❌ Out"</code> grants
        <code>@@tank</code> to whoever picks Tank <i>and</i> lets only <code>@@raiders</code> pick
        it (the grant mention goes before <code>only:</code>, the restriction after).
        <code>/poll create restrict-to:</code> does the same for voting, and
        <code>/edit restrict-to:</code> / <code>clear-restriction:true</code> change or remove one.
        The event creator and server managers always pass. A restriction only gates entry: losing
        the role later never costs anyone their seat, and a role that gets deleted simply stops
        counting. Restrictions are set from Discord only — the web can remove one, not add one.
    </li>
</ul>
<Callout Variant="CalloutVariant.Caution">
    Don't use a standing interest role as an <code>attendee-role</code>: CalCrony removes the
    attendee role from everyone when the event ends, which would wipe your group's membership.
    Keep the two kinds separate — interest roles for reach, attendee roles for attendance.
    (The bot needs <b>Manage Roles</b> — the current invite link grants it — and you must drag
    roles the bot manages <b>below the bot's own role</b> in the role list yourself; role
    ordering is something no invite can set.)
</Callout>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4 mb-2">Worked example: weekly D&amp;D night</h2>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    From zero to a self-running game night:
</p>
<ol class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    <li class="mb-1">Create two roles: <code>@@dnd</code> (interest — let players self-assign) and
        <code>@@session-players</code> (empty; CalCrony will manage it).</li>
    <li class="mb-1"><code>/availability role role:@@dnd when:"friday 7pm" duration:180</code> —
        check the group's calendars before committing to a slot (players link theirs once with
        <code>/calendar connect</code>).</li>
    <li class="mb-1"><code>/create title:"D&amp;D Night" when:"friday 7pm" duration:180
        repeat:weekly attendee-role:@@session-players thread:true</code> — a recurring event with
        RSVP buttons, a discussion thread that Going RSVPers join automatically, and an
        auto-managed attendee role.</li>
    <li class="mb-1"><code>/notify event:"D&amp;D Night" minutes-before:60 mention:@@dnd</code> —
        the whole interest group gets a one-hour heads-up; add a second one mentioning
        <code>@@session-players</code> at 10 minutes for just the confirmed table.</li>
    <li class="mb-1"><code>/template save name:"Game Night" event:"D&amp;D Night"</code> — next
        campaign or a one-shot, start from <code>/create template:"Game Night"</code> and only
        pick a time.</li>
    <li class="mb-1">Skip a holiday week with <code>/series skip</code>; the next occurrence posts
        itself when each session ends. <code>/link</code> gives everyone a calendar-app
        subscription that carries the whole schedule.</li>
</ol>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    The same recipe fits movie nights, raid schedules, or book clubs — swap the roles and the
    repeat rule.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4 mb-3">Slash commands</h2>
<div class="table-responsive border rounded">
    <table class="table table-sm mb-0 align-middle">
        <thead>
            <tr><th class="meta-mono text-uppercase">Command</th><th class="meta-mono text-uppercase">What it does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>/create</code></td><td>Create an event — <code>when</code> is natural language ("tomorrow 6pm"); <code>repeat</code> options make it recurring (<code>repeat:weekly repeat-days:tue,thu</code> or <code>repeat:weekly repeat-days:weekdays</code> for day sets — <code>repeat-days</code> always needs <code>repeat:weekly</code>); <code>attendee-role</code> grants a role to attending RSVPs until the event ends; <code>thread</code> opens a discussion thread on the event message; <code>rsvp-options:"⚔️ Raider x10, ❌ Out"</code> customizes the RSVP buttons (the first entry — or one marked with a trailing <code>*</code> — counts as attending; <code>xN</code> caps it; mentioning a role gives that option its own attendee role; <code>only: @@role</code> at the end limits who may pick it); <code>attendee-limit</code> caps attendance (extras join a waitlist and move up automatically); <code>rsvp-close:"2h before"</code> stops RSVP changes before start; <code>restrict-to:@@role</code> limits every option to holders of the named roles; <code>multi-rsvp:true</code> lets each member pick more than one option (clicking a chosen option again removes it; no option is exclusive, so choose options that combine sensibly)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/list</code> · <code>/edit</code> · <code>/delete</code></td><td>Browse and manage events (creator or server manager); repeating events ask for a <code>scope</code>; <code>/edit</code> can change or clear the attendee role, change or clear the signup restriction (<code>restrict-to</code> / <code>clear-restriction</code> — existing RSVPs stay), reshape <code>rsvp-options</code> (same labels keep their RSVPs, and each entry's role and restriction are re-applied from what you send), set or clear <code>attendee-limit</code> / <code>rsvp-close</code>, and switch <code>multi-rsvp</code> on or off (off is refused while anyone holds more than one RSVP)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/series skip</code> · <code>/series stop</code> · <code>/series info</code> · <code>/series edit</code></td><td>Skip the next occurrence · stop repeating · see the schedule · change the rule (editing an ended series revives it)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/remind</code> · <code>/notify</code></td><td>One-off reminders · scheduled pings before an event starts</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/availability role|event</code></td><td>Free/busy grid for a role or an event's Going list</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/calendar connect</code></td><td>Link your Google Calendar (free/busy scope only)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/link</code></td><td>Your server's calendar-subscribe URL</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/poll create</code> · <code>/poll time</code></td><td>Polls with live vote bars — <code>time</code> pits candidate times against each other; <code>restrict-to:@@role</code> limits voting and voter-added options to the role's holders</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/poll close</code> · <code>/poll convert</code></td><td>Close voting · turn a time poll's winning slot into an event</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/template save</code> · <code>/template list</code> · <code>/template edit</code> · <code>/template delete</code></td><td>Save an event's setup (content, reminders, repeat rule) for reuse with <code>/create template:</code>; edit or delete it later (creator or manager — reminder lists are edited on the web Templates tab)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/settings</code> · <code>/timestamp</code></td><td>Timezones, the personal DM-reminders opt-in (<code>dm-reminders</code>, off by default), default channel, native-event mirroring, the public calendar link (<code>public-calendar on|off|new-link</code>) · Discord timestamp helper</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>/help</code></td><td>What CalCrony is, first steps, and links to docs, the community server, and support</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4 mb-2">The web app</h2>
<ul class="text-body-secondary">
    <li><b>Sign in</b> with Discord — CalCrony only asks for your identity and server list, never your messages.</li>
    <li><b>RSVP from any page</b>: your choice syncs to the Discord embed within about 15 seconds.</li>
    <li><b>Link your Google Calendar</b> under My settings; the availability grid on each event shows who's free.</li>
    <li><b>Subscribe</b> to a server's calendar from its settings page — works with Google, Apple, and Outlook. Repeating events carry their full schedule, so your calendar shows every future occurrence.</li>
    <li><b>DM reminders</b> (off by default): turn them on under My settings to also get event reminders and start pings by DM for events you're attending. Only you can enable it, and if your DMs are closed to the bot it switches itself off.</li>
    <li><b>Public calendar</b> (managers; off by default): turn it on under Server settings to get a login-free link to a month view of the server's events — titles, start times, durations, locations, and channel names only — never descriptions or RSVPs — and search engines are asked not to index it. Generate a new link any time to retire the old one.</li>
    <li><b>Run polls</b> from the Polls tab: create, vote, add options, close, and convert a time poll's winner into an event — all synced to the Discord embed.</li>
    <li><b>Templates</b>: save any event's setup from its page, browse them on the Templates tab, and start new events prefilled from one.</li>
    <li><b>Activity log &amp; CSV export</b> (managers): the Activity tab shows who created, edited, deleted, or skipped events, changed repeat schedules, ran polls, saved templates, or changed server settings — from Discord or the web — filterable by action and member, kept for 90 days. The same tab's <b>Export events (CSV)</b> button downloads every event with its RSVPs (one row per RSVP, custom options and waitlist status included) for spreadsheets.</li>
    <li><b>Custom RSVPs, limits &amp; waitlists</b>: the event form lets you define your own RSVP buttons (any emoji + label), mark which one counts as <i>attending</i>, and give it a capacity — RSVPs past the cap queue on a waitlist shown on the event page and the Discord embed, and the first in line is seated and pinged automatically when a spot frees. An optional "RSVPs close" cutoff ("2h before" or an absolute time) freezes RSVP changes before start. Tick <b>Allow more than one RSVP per member</b> and members can pick several options at once (Tank <i>and</i> Healer) — clicking a selected one removes it, only the attending option keeps a waitlist, and turning it back off is refused while anyone holds more than one. No option is exclusive (with the default Going / Not going / Maybe set a member could hold Going <i>and</i> Not going), so pair it with choices that make sense together.</li>
    <li><b>Attendee roles</b>: events created with one (via <code>/create attendee-role:</code>, or a role mentioned per option inside <code>rsvp-options</code>) grant it to whoever is seated on that option within ~15 seconds, swap it when someone switches options, and remove it when the event ends. The event page lists every option that grants a role; picking them happens in Discord.</li>
    <li><b>Role-restricted signup</b>: options and polls limited by role show a 🔒 chip naming the roles, and the buttons stay clickable. The web app can't see your Discord roles, so the API answers from role snapshots the bot keeps — and if it can't confirm them (the bot has been offline for more than half an hour, or the restriction was just created) it refuses and asks you to RSVP or vote from Discord rather than guessing. Creators and managers always pass. The edit form can remove a restriction; setting one happens in Discord with <code>restrict-to:</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Event threads</b>: check "Create a discussion thread" when creating an event and a thread opens on its Discord message — "Going" RSVPers are added automatically, and it archives when the event ends.</li>
    <li><b>Repeating events</b>: pick a schedule when creating (daily/weekly/monthly — by date or nth weekday — or yearly); weekly schedules can tick a set of days (Tue + Thu, or the Weekdays preset) and still repeat every N weeks. When one occurrence ends the next posts automatically. Skip or stop from the event page; edits ask whether they apply to one occurrence or the whole series.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4 mb-2">Good to know</h2>
<Callout Variant="CalloutVariant.Note">
    <b>Calendar subscriptions refresh on the calendar app's schedule, not ours.</b> The feed itself
    updates the instant an event changes, but Google Calendar re-fetches URL subscriptions only every
    12–24 hours (with no manual refresh); Apple Calendar and Outlook let you choose a faster
    interval. If a new event seems missing from your calendar, it's almost certainly waiting on the
    next fetch — opening the subscribe URL that <code>/link</code> gives you always shows the
    current feed.
</Callout>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    When connecting Google Calendar you may see a <i>"Google hasn't verified this app"</i> screen —
    click <b>Advanced</b>, then <b>Continue</b>; the permission requested is free/busy only (see the
    <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>). If you ever see a 🔄 "needs to reconnect" badge,
    re-running <code>/calendar connect</code> or clicking Connect in My settings fixes it in seconds.
    Server lists are a snapshot from sign-in; the "re-sync servers" link in the sidebar refreshes
    yours instantly.
</p>
<p class="text-body-secondary" style="max-width:66ch">
    Stuck, or have an idea? Join the
    <a href="https://discord.gg/aEdYyZYgyV" target="_blank" rel="noopener">community Discord server</a> —
